    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0d78a19f_be2f84c2",
        "filename": "floss/pandora/server/l2cap.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 116,
      "author": {
        "id": 2052731
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-25T21:59:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I checked how callbacks behave when I worked on the L2CAP and RFCOMM profiles. I saw that the callback for FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result and other callbacks in the socket_manager always run their code completely before moving to this L2CAP callback. This means the connecting_sockets dictionary always gets filled with data before reaching this callback. Based on this, I decided to use connecting_sockets and listening_sockets from socket_manager instead of duplicating the code. Based on your comments, I will create getter methods for both of them in socket_manager. Is this approach good for you based on my description or do you still prefer to clone the code from socket_manager?\n@chharry@chromium.org",
      "parentUuid": "3267f6b2_dbc5c779",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 116,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "e0e8d4410383426be345acdf925303701aba6f8b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fc399497_e084b1b3",
        "filename": "floss/pandora/server/l2cap.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 116,
      "author": {
        "id": 2052731
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-25T21:59:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Would you prefer I create a getter function in FlossSocketManagerClient or clone the callback code from FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result directly here?",
      "parentUuid": "528bf2e1_998b10ef",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 42,
        "endLine": 116,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "e0e8d4410383426be345acdf925303701aba6f8b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2a1fd9ff_9f8fa54c",
        "filename": "floss/pandora/server/l2cap.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 116,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-26T02:26:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I saw that the callback for `FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result` and other callbacks in the socket_manager always run their code completely before moving to this L2CAP callback.\n\nSo `FlossSocketManagerClient` adds `self` as an observer just like what `register_callback_observer` does for the other instances (such as `CreateL2CAPObserver` here). Although after python 3.7 the iteration order is guaranteed to be the insertion order, in the API layer they are both Floss clients so it doesn\u0027t make sense to assume the execution order of their callbacks.",
      "parentUuid": "0d78a19f_be2f84c2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 116,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "e0e8d4410383426be345acdf925303701aba6f8b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "52436c85_3d10a845",
        "filename": "floss/pandora/server/l2cap.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 116,
      "author": {
        "id": 2052731
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-27T21:58:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I introduced getter and setter methods for both `connecting_sockets` and `listening_sockets` within the `FlossSocketManagerClient`.",
      "parentUuid": "fc399497_e084b1b3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 42,
        "endLine": 116,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "e0e8d4410383426be345acdf925303701aba6f8b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e1bf9075_27dc7a62",
        "filename": "floss/pandora/server/l2cap.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 116,
      "author": {
        "id": 2052731
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-27T21:58:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To ensure that `socket_info` is generated only when necessary and to avoid the redundancy of creating file descriptors (fd) that go unused, I implemented a check using getter methods to determine if `connecting_id` is already present in `connecting_sockets`. This check helps us understand if `socket_info` has been filled by executing the `FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result` observer. If we directly replicate the implementation from socket_manager without this check, it results in overwriting the `connecting_id` and adding a new fd value, potentially leading to unused fds and memory issues.\n\nTherefore, if `connecting_id` does not exist, I proceed to use the clone code from `FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result` and then update `connecting_sockets` with the obtained information using the setter method. This approach is also applied to `listening_sockets`.\n\nI suggest incorporating a check for `connecting_id` within `FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result`. This is because if `L2CAP.on_outgoing_connection_result` is executed first, it populates `connecting_sockets`, and then running `FlossSocketManagerClient.on_outgoing_connection_result` might overwrite `connecting_id` values, leading to the unnecessary creation of fds. What do you think, please?",
      "parentUuid": "2a1fd9ff_9f8fa54c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 116,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "e0e8d4410383426be345acdf925303701aba6f8b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
